<h1>Matrix Cases Exclusion</h1>
<p>A full Cartesian product usually contains combinations that make no sense for the application under test.
	<strong>Exclusion rules</strong> remove them in the test source, before they ever become cases. This page follows
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/">Filtering Matrix Dimensions</a>, which narrows a
	matrix from the controller, and uses the <strong>Cases Exclusion</strong> test of the same
	<strong>Advanced Configuration Parameters</strong> group.</p>
<p>The difference between the two is where the decision lives. A dimension filter is made in the run panel for one
	configuration and can be lifted again. An exclusion rule is part of the test's matrix declaration: every run of the
	test, from the GUI, the CLI or a CI pipeline, leaves those combinations out.</p>
<p>Select the <strong>Cases Exclusion</strong> test. It runs the Maze application with a command and a mix of
	optional arguments, so it needs both the <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> and a
	<strong>Maze Save/Load File</strong> for the commands that save or load a maze. Set both, for example to the
	<code>v3.0.0</code> folder and its <code>maze.json</code>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-cases-exclusion/cases-exclusion-config.png", "Cases Exclusion run panel with the Maze Executable Folder and Maze Save/Load File set, reporting Total Cases 61 and a Test Cases group header reading 61 of 61", "large") }}
<p>The panel reports <strong>Total Cases: 61</strong> and the group header reads <strong>Test Cases (61/61)</strong>.
	Six dimensions with <code>4 × 4 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2</code> values would produce 256 combinations; the matrix produces
	61 because the rest are excluded. Nothing marks that in the panel. Unlike a filtered dimension, whose column header
	is flagged as changed, an excluded combination was never part of the matrix, so there is nothing to lift and nothing
	to badge. The execution tree's <strong>Plan</strong> column shows the same 61.</p>

<h2 id="defining-exclusion-rules">Defining exclusion rules</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_cases_exclusion/") }}
<p>The <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/#matrix"><code>Matrix</code></a> declaration takes an
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/#exclusion"><code>exclude</code></a> list next to its dimensions.
	Each entry is either a dict of values to match or a lambda over the case:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('matrix = OrcraftConfigField.Matrix(
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("command", ("generate", "solve", "save", "load")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("algorithm", (None, "BFS", "DFS", "ASTAR")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("seed", (None, 42)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("height", (None, 5)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("width", (None, 10)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("use_file", (False, True)),

	exclude=[
		{"command": "generate", "algorithm": "BFS"},
		{"command": "generate", "algorithm": "DFS"},
		{"command": "generate", "algorithm": "ASTAR"},
		{"command": "generate", "use_file": True},
		{"command": "load", "use_file": False},
		lambda case: case.params["command"] in ("save", "load") and case.params["algorithm"] is not None,
		lambda case: case.params["command"] == "load" and any(case.params[p] is not None for p in ("seed", "height", "width")),
		lambda case: case.params["command"] == "solve" and case.params["use_file"] and any(case.params[p] is not None for p in ("seed", "height", "width")),
	],
)') }}
<p>A value of <code>None</code> in a dimension means the argument is not passed to the Maze application at all.
	The run panel shows such a value as an empty cell, the log table as <code>-</code>, and the Details dialog as
	<code>None</code>.</p>

<h3 id="dict-rules">Dict rules</h3>
<p>A dict rule names one value per dimension it cares about and excludes every case that matches all of them.
	<code>{"command": "generate", "use_file": True}</code> removes every <code>generate</code> case that would also
	pass a file, whatever its algorithm, seed, height or width. A dict rule cannot express "any value but this one",
	which is why <code>generate</code> needs three rules to exclude the three real algorithms while leaving
	<code>None</code> in.</p>

<h3 id="lambda-rules">Lambda rules</h3>
<p>A lambda receives the candidate case and excludes it by returning <code>True</code>. It can compare several
	dimensions, test for <code>None</code>, and use <code>in</code> or <code>any</code> over a set of keys. The rule
	<code>case.params["command"] in ("save", "load") and case.params["algorithm"] is not None</code> replaces six
	dict rules, one per command and algorithm pair, with a single line. The last rule keeps <code>solve</code> from
	both loading a maze from a file and asking for a freshly generated one.</p>
<p>Both kinds can be mixed in the same list, and a case is excluded as soon as any rule matches it. What survives is
	the product of the values kept for each command:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>generate</strong> - no algorithm and no file, so only seed, height and width vary: 8 cases.</li>
	<li><strong>solve</strong> - every algorithm with generation arguments and no file, plus every algorithm with a
		file and no generation arguments: 36 cases.</li>
	<li><strong>save</strong> - no algorithm, everything else free: 16 cases.</li>
	<li><strong>load</strong> - a file and nothing else: 1 case.</li>
</ul>
<p>That is the 61 the panel reports. Excluded combinations are not skipped at run time. They are never planned, so
	the case count the controller shows before a run is the case count that runs.</p>

<h3 id="using-the-surviving-cases">Using the surviving cases</h3>
<p>The test body is the same shape as in <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/">Matrix and
	Cases</a>. It receives only the cases that passed the rules, so it does not have to guard against the excluded
	combinations itself:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	maze_app = MazeApp(
		self.config.maze_executable_folder,
		case.params["command"],
		width=case.params["width"],
		height=case.params["height"],
		seed=case.params["seed"],
		algorithm=case.params["algorithm"],
		maze_file=self.config.maze_file if case.params["use_file"] else None,
	)

	maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p>The <code>use_file</code> dimension decides whether the configured <strong>Maze Save/Load File</strong> is
	passed along. Because the rules already removed <code>load</code> without a file and <code>generate</code> with
	one, the body can pass the file whenever the dimension says so.</p>

<h2 id="running-the-surviving-cases">Running the surviving cases</h2>
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> to commit the two configured paths, then
	<strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>. The test row reports 61 planned,
	done and passed cases, and the log's result table lists every surviving combination in matrix order:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-cases-exclusion/running-cases-exclusion-test.png", "Completed Cases Exclusion run with all 61 cases passing, the log listing the last save and load cases and the test result summary", "large") }}
<p>The order of the table follows the dimensions as declared: all <code>generate</code> cases first, then
	<code>solve</code>, then <code>save</code>, and the single <code>load</code> case last. Within a command the
	later dimensions change fastest, exactly as they would without exclusion, only with the removed rows missing.</p>

<h3 id="inspecting-the-run">Inspecting the run</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the completed run. The <strong>List Dimensions</strong> table on the left
	still lists every value each dimension declares, including the ones no surviving case uses together, because the
	rules act on combinations rather than on values. The case table below it and the results tree on the right hold
	the 61 cases that exist:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-cases-exclusion/cases-exclusion-details.png", "Cases Exclusion Details view showing the declared dimension values, the 61 planned and run cases and a results tree with one passing case cycle under each case", "large") }}
<p>An exclusion rule therefore changes what the matrix is, not how it is shown. Filters and case selection in the run
	panel still apply on top of it, so a reader can narrow the 61 cases further for one configuration in the same way
	as on any other matrix test.</p>

<p>All the dimensions on this page list their values by hand. The next page,
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/">Matrix Dimensions Randomization</a>,
	lets a dimension draw its values at random and shows how the controller keeps such a matrix plannable.</p>
